WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.840
- Five.

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:05.460
"Three. Two. One. Pull up."

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.180
- I'm experiencing 1.8gs
right now, which means I weigh

00:00:10.186 --> 00:00:12.381
about 1.8 times what I normally would.

00:00:12.381 --> 00:00:15.720
I've been instructed to keep my head
front and centre to avoid nausea.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.640
But all this is about to change

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.520
because this is the
European Space Agency's zero-g flight.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:21.940
Those are students doing research.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:23.800
This is Neil, who's here for my safety.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.660
And we're about to go into freefall.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:27.834
Whoa!

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.360
Whoa!

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.917
And here's the confusing thing.

00:00:32.917 --> 00:00:35.534
We are still going up right now.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.640
That camera is stabilised to
the actual horizon,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.560
and it's not until 10 seconds into
our 20 second parabola,

00:00:42.561 --> 00:00:46.252
into our manoeuvre, that we
clip the apex and start heading

00:00:46.252 --> 00:00:48.669
back down towards the ground.

00:00:49.668 --> 00:00:51.585
And here we go, 1.8gs.

00:00:53.514 --> 00:00:57.215
- In this special aircraft,
we divide the three axes.

00:00:57.215 --> 00:00:59.849
There is pitch, roll and thrust,

00:00:59.849 --> 00:01:02.898
and each pilot flies one axis.

00:01:02.898 --> 00:01:06.039
In every other aircraft, one pilot
flies the three axes and

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.940
the other one manages the systems,
talks on the radio, and so on.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:16.340
To fly parabolas, we need to
fly only one axis by one pilot.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:19.036
We try to avoid any turbulence.

00:01:19.036 --> 00:01:22.713
Any turbulence is a nightmare for us.

00:01:22.713 --> 00:01:25.772
- The two control sticks,
just like any plane,

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.760
are completely locked together,
so they always move at the same time.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.700
So one of the pilots is using

00:01:31.715 --> 00:01:34.521
effectively elastic bands
on either side of his stick

00:01:34.521 --> 00:01:38.415
so that he doesn't affect
the axis of the other pilot.

00:01:38.415 --> 00:01:41.285
One of them does the pitch,
the other one does the roll

00:01:41.285 --> 00:01:43.597
whilst trying not to upset the pitch.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.700
- In 1971, the Apollo 15 astronauts
tested something on the moon:

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.720
The idea that all objects,
no matter their weight,

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:52.427
fall at the same speed
if there's no air.

00:01:52.427 --> 00:01:55.740
They used a hammer and a feather,
and they landed at the same time.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.256
ASTRONAUT: "How about that?"

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.320
- But the weirdest thing is that
if that astronaut had thrown that hammer,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.495
it still would have been in free fall

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.120
from the moment it left his glove
to the moment it hit the ground.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.540
The only pull on it is from
the gravity of the moon.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:10.060
The maths work out the same

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:13.151
no matter what speed or direction
it's going relative to us.

00:02:13.151 --> 00:02:15.032
That's what we just did here.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.660
If it sounds weird, sure,
but we just proved it.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.720
And we're about to prove it again.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.580
- Every five parabolas
or ten parabolas, we change

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.780
to maintain the best accuracy
of the parabolas, especially

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:31.500
on the pitch axis because
it's the most demanding flying technique.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.497
So after five or ten parabolas,
you are so tired and you

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:40.620
have to go on to another
less demanding axis.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.920
PILOT: "3, 2, 1, pull up."

00:02:43.924 --> 00:02:46.809
- This parabolic manoeuvre
isn't the normal kind of thing

00:02:46.809 --> 00:02:49.579
that you do in an Airbus, and
the software in this plane

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:53.900
hasn't been modified because to do so
would be a huge effort to re-qualify it.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.692
With the normal software,
you have the normal alarm

00:02:56.692 --> 00:02:59.476
saying, "This doesn't seem
like a very good idea, guys,"

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.360
and one of the pilots that
isn't too busy on the other two axes

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.760
is turning off the alarms
that we don't need to worry about

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.920
and keeping track of the ones
we might need to worry about.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.480
PILOT: "Injection."

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.360
- So those pilots are
keeping us on the exact path

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.224
that an object would take
if you hurled it into the sky

00:03:17.224 --> 00:03:21.004
at hundreds of kilometres per hour
and there was no air.

00:03:21.004 --> 00:03:24.960
They're giving us this little protective bubble
of zero-g to fly around in.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.980
There is no terminal velocity here,
only the design limitations of the plane.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.016
And we're going to hit those pretty
soon, along with something else.

00:03:32.016 --> 00:03:34.940
Because when you're descending
this fast towards the ground,

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.680
the Earth approaches pretty quickly.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.100
Thank you, Neil!

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:42.620
If you're a Masters or PhD
student from an ESA member state,

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:44.900
and you want to be
one of the people back there,

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.120
then have a look at the
Fly Your Thesis program.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.000
The link is in the description.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:51.980
Thank you, Neil!

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.440
I got it, I got it, we're good.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.040
And down.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:02.880
Oooph.

